## DOM tree structure

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head><title>Hello</title></head>
  <body>
    <h1 id="title">Hello World</h1>
    <p class="intro">Welcome.</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

Tree:
```
Document
└── html
    ├── head
    │   └── title → "Hello"
    └── body
        ├── h1 #title → "Hello World"
        └── p .intro → "Welcome."
```

## Querying the DOM

```js
const heading = document.getElementById('title');
const first   = document.querySelector('.intro');
const items   = document.querySelectorAll('li');
```

## Manipulating the DOM

```js
// Change text
heading.textContent = 'Updated Title';

// Change attributes
heading.setAttribute('class', 'active');
heading.classList.add('highlight');

// Create and insert nodes
const p = document.createElement('p');
p.textContent = 'New paragraph';
document.body.appendChild(p);

// Remove
heading.remove();
```

## DOM vs HTML source

The DOM is live — JavaScript mutations and the browser's own error correction make it diverge from the raw HTML source. `View Source` shows the original HTML; DevTools Elements panel shows the current DOM state.

## Virtual DOM (React context)

React's Virtual DOM is a lightweight in-memory copy of the real DOM. React diffs the virtual tree and applies only the minimal required changes to the real DOM — avoiding expensive full repaints.

